  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 253 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nUpper low continues to move northeast this afternoon and \nevening, with widespread rain observed across much of eastern \nNorth Dakota, west central Minnesota, and northwest Minnesota. \nWinds have started to shift to the northeast, and will continue \nto become more northerly through the evening hours and \novernight. The upper low slowly exits the area late Tuesday into\nWednesday morning, with breezy north winds expected through \nmuch of Tuesday. Much cooler air pours into the Northern Plains,\nwith temperatures close to average values Tuesday through \nFriday. Our next chance for precipitation will be late Saturday \ninto Sunday as a fast moving trough moves across the area.\n\n...Heavy Rain Through Wednesday...\n\nModerate to heavy rain will continue through the remainder of \nthe afternoon and evening across much of eastern North Dakota, \nnorthwest Minnesota, and west central Minnesota. An upper low is\nslowly moving to the northeast, but is expected to occlude and \nbriefly become cut off from the upper flow as we go through the \nday Tuesday. As this happens, there could be a slight westward \nshift of the system. This could keep light rain in the forecast \nthrough much of Tuesday and into early Wednesday morning, \nespecially east of the Red River Valley. At this time, there is \nabout a 75 percent chance to see an additional 0.25-0.50 inch in\nthe central Red River Valley, with higher chances further east.\nSome locales could see upwards of 3 inches by Wednesday \nmorning, with the best chances being in west central Minnesota \n(25 percent chance). \n\n...Breezy North Winds Tuesday...\n\nNorth winds fill into the area as the H5 low moves slowly \neastward. These winds will increase this evening and overnight, \nwith 15-25 mph sustained winds expected by early Tuesday \nmorning. Gusts up to 40 mph are possible, especially in the \ncentral and southern Red River Valley. Pressure rises are \nexpected to be sufficient to favor a few higher gusts; however, \nwith 700-850mb winds in the 35-45 knot range, and generally weak\ncold air advection, there is minimal support for anything more \nthan occasional advisory level gusts.",
